module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Basic
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Homology.ShortComplex.ShortExact
public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Abelian.Basic
public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.EssentiallySmall
public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Linear.LinearFunctor
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.FiniteDimensional.Defs
import Mathlib.Algebra.Homology.ShortComplex.ExactFunctor
import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Free
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dimension.Finrank

/-!
# Subobjects generated by subsets of finite fibres

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Section 9(d), printed p.175, the paragraph
immediately before Lemma 9.33 (blueprint: lem-sfb-fibre-generated-subobject).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w r s

namespace CategoryTheory

/-- Every subset of a finite-dimensional fibre generates a least subobject, expressed
by its unique factorization property among monomorphisms into the given object.

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Section 9(d), printed p.175, the paragraph
beginning "Let X be an object of C and S a subset"
(blueprint: lem-sfb-fibre-generated-subobject).

Proof plan:
1. Faithfulness reflects monomorphisms and epimorphisms by cancellation. In an abelian
category (`def-sp-abelian`), a morphism that is both is invertible: write it as a kernel,
use epimorphy to show the next map is zero, and factor the identity through the kernel.
Additivity and preservation of short exact sequences imply preservation of kernels,
cokernels and finite biproducts (`lem-sp-exact-functor-preserves-homology`).
2. Among monomorphisms whose fibre images contain S, choose i : G ⟶ V minimizing
`Module.finrank k (F.obj G)` (`def-weil-vector-space-dimension`). The identity is a candidate.
3. For another candidate j : H ⟶ V, form the kernel P of (i,-j) : G ⊞ H ⟶ V.
Its projections p : P ⟶ G and q : P ⟶ H are monic, with p ≫ i = q ≫ j.
Preservation of the kernel and biproduct identifies F(P) with pairs of lifts having the
same image. Every element of S has such a pair, so p ≫ i is again a candidate and
minimality gives dim F(G) ≤ dim F(P).
4. Apply exactness to the cokernel sequence of p. Dimension additivity
(`lem-trf-finite-dimensional-short-exact-dimension`) gives
 dim F(G) = dim F(P) + dim F(coker p), so the last summand is zero.
Thus F(p) is bijective, and reflection of isomorphisms makes p invertible.
5. The composite p⁻¹ ≫ q factors i through j, and cancellation by j gives uniqueness.
For two least candidates their unique arrows in both directions compose to identities,
yielding the unique isomorphism over V. Empty S and zero V are included throughout.
-/
theorem exists_fibre_generated_subobject
    {k : Type r} [Field k] {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] [Abelian C]
    [Linear k C] [EssentiallySmall.{w} C]
    (F : C ⥤ ModuleCat.{s} k) [F.Additive] [F.Linear k] [F.Faithful]
    (h_exact : ∀ T : ShortComplex C, T.ShortExact → (T.map F).ShortExact)
    (h_finite : ∀ X : C, FiniteDimensional k (F.obj X))
    (V : C) (S : Set (F.obj V)) :
    ∃ (G : C) (i : G ⟶ V), Mono i ∧ S ⊆ Set.range (F.map i) ∧
      ∀ (H : C) (j : H ⟶ V), Mono j → S ⊆ Set.range (F.map j) →
        ∃! a : G ⟶ H, a ≫ j = i := by
  sorry

end CategoryTheory
